{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KcxgbB3mgSs3k8fFwswSZ2","type":"tool_result","content":"// Package limits is the controller's book of exhausted usage limits: the\n// reactive half of subscription limit handling (#81). When a job stops at a\n// limit, the Reaper's Finisher records it here and the pool starts no agent\n// job for the exhausted account or model family until it resets. Observed\n// readings, thresholds and status comments are #63's.\n//\n// The book is persisted in the controller database's meta table, so a pause\n// survives a restart. It never kills a running job.\npackage limits\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/state\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\t// DefaultPause is the first pause when the CLI gave no reset time.\n\tDefaultPause = 30 * time.Minute\n\t// BackoffCap is the longest pause chosen without a reset time. Each\n\t// further stop with no reset doubles the pause up to this; a job that\n\t// succeeds resets it.\n\tBackoffCap = 4 * time.Hour\n\t// MaxPause clamps a reset time that is far away: a misread unit must not\n\t// block the account for years.\n\tMaxPause = 8 * 24 * time.Hour\n\n\tmetaKey = \"limits.exhausted\"\n)\n\n// Meta is the key-value store the book persists in (*state.Store).\ntype Meta interface {\n\tMeta(ctx context.Context, key string) (string, error) // errors.Is(err, state.ErrNotFound) = empty book\n\tSetMeta(ctx context.Context, key, value string) error\n}\n\n// entry is one exhausted family. Family \"\" is the whole account.\ntype entry struct {\n\tUntil int64 `json:\"until\"`          // Unix seconds\n\tStep  int   `json:\"step,omitempty\"` // consecutive stops with no reset time\n}\n\n// Book records which families are exhausted and until when.\ntype Book struct {\n\tmu      sync.Mutex\n\tentries map[string]entry\n\tmeta    Meta\n\tnow     func() time.Time\n\tlog     *slog.Logger\n}\n\n// Open loads the book. A missing key gives an empty book; a malformed value\n// logs WARN limits.corrupt and gives an empty book (the next Exhaust\n// overwrites it). Only a read error from meta is returned.\nfunc Open(ctx context.Context, m Meta, now func() time.Time, log *slog.Logger) (*Book, error) {\n\tif now == nil {\n\t\tnow = time.Now\n\t}\n\tif log == nil {\n\t\tlog = slog.Default()\n\t}\n\tb := &Book{entries: map[string]entry{}, meta: m, now: now, log: log}\n\tv, err := m.Meta(ctx, metaKey)\n\tswitch {\n\tcase errors.Is(err, state.ErrNotFound):\n\t\treturn b, nil\n\tcase err != nil:\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"open limits book: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tvar got map[string]entry\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(v), &got); err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Warn(\"limits.corrupt\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn b, nil\n\t}\n\tif got != nil {\n\t\tb.entries = got\n\t}\n\treturn b, nil\n}\n\nfunc backoff(step int) time.Duration {\n\td := DefaultPause\n\tfor i := 0; i < step && d < BackoffCap; i++ {\n\t\td *= 2\n\t}\n\treturn min(d, BackoffCap)\n}\n\n// Exhaust records that family (or the whole account when \"\") is out of quota\n// and returns the effective time it is blocked until.\n//\n// A resetsAt in the future is used as given, clamped to MaxPause. A zero or\n// past resetsAt means the CLI gave no reset: the pause is DefaultPause, then\n// doubles with each further stop to BackoffCap, but only once the previous\n// pause has run out. A stop recorded while the family is already paused (a job\n// that was running when the limit hit) changes nothing, so Exhaust is\n// idempotent. The later of the old and new times is kept, and the book is\n// persisted before Exhaust returns; on a persist error memory is unchanged.\nfunc (b *Book) Exhaust(ctx context.Context, family string, resetsAt time.Time) (time.Time, error) {\n\tb.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer b.mu.Unlock()\n\tnow := b.now()\n\told, had := b.entries[family]\n\toldUntil := time.Unix(old.Until, 0)\n\tnext := old\n\tvar until time.Time\n\tswitch {\n\tcase resetsAt.After(now):\n\t\tuntil = resetsAt\n\t\tif limit := now.Add(MaxPause); until.After(limit) {\n\t\t\tuntil = limit\n\t\t}\n\t\tnext.Step = 0\n// reaperLoop returns the Reaper loop, or nil when the executor cannot be built.\nfunc reaperLoop(w *wiring) controller.Loop {\n\t_, r, err := w.reaperParts()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tw.Log.Error(\"reaper.executor_unavailable\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\treturn r\n}\n\n// resumeCloseouts is the closeout-resume startup hook. It runs before the pool\n// is adopted, so it never touches the pool.\nfunc (w *wiring) resumeCloseouts(ctx context.Context) error {\n\t// A store that cannot be read means state is broken: fail startup.\n\tif _, err := w.limitsBook(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"closeout-resume: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\t_, r, err := w.reaperParts()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\t// Without an executor nothing runs jobs either; do not keep serve down.\n\t\tw.Log.Error(\"reaper.resume_skipped\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\treturn r.ResumeAll(ctx)\n}\n\n// parseFunc reads a job's agent result from its stdout log, with the adapter\n// its role is configured for.\nfunc (w *wiring) parseFunc() reaper.ParseFunc {\n\treg, err := agent.NewRegistry(claude.New(claude.Options{}))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tpanic(err) // a duplicate adapter name is a programming error\n\t}\n\treturn func(ctx context.Context, cfg *config.Resolved, sj state.Job, h executor.Handle, exitCode int) (agent.Result, error) {\n\t\tif cfg == nil {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, errors.New(\"no config loaded\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar kind config.Kind\n\t\tswitch sj.Stage {\n\t\tcase closeout.StageTriage:\n\t\t\tkind = config.KindTriage\n\t\tcase closeout.StageWorker:\n\t\t\tkind = config.KindWorker\n\t\tcase closeout.StageReview:\n\t\t\tkind = config.KindReviewer\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, fmt.Errorf(\"stage %q has no agent\", sj.Stage)\n\t\t}\n\t\trole, ok := cfg.Role(kind, sj.Role)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, fmt.Errorf(\"role %s/%s is not configured\", kind, sj.Role)\n\t\t}\n\t\ta, err := reg.Get(role.Agent)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif h.StdoutPath == \"\" {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, errors.New(\"job has no stdout log\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tf, err := os.Open(h.StdoutPath)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, fmt.Errorf(\"open stdout log: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tdefer f.Close()\n\t\tres, err := a.Parse(f, exitCode)\n\t\tif errors.Is(err, agent.ErrNoResult) {\n\t\t\treturn res, nil // a killed job has no result event: the partial result is the answer\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn res, err\n\t}\n}\n93-// LimitHit says the run met a usage or rate limit. Consumers act on it only\n94-// when !Result.OK: a run that saw a rejection and then succeeded keeps it.\n95:type LimitHit struct {\n96-\tScope    LimitScope\n97-\tFamily   string    // \"opus\" | \"sonnet\" | \"\" (whole account or unknown)\n98-\tResetsAt time.Time // UTC; zero when the stream gave no machine-readable reset\n99-\tSignal   LimitSignal\n100-}\n101-\n102-// Usage is token counts. Missing or null fields count as 0.\n103-type Usage struct{ Input, Output, CacheWrite, CacheRead int64 }\n104-\n105-// Add returns the field-wise sum.\n106-func (u Usage) Add(o Usage) Usage {\n107-\treturn Usage{u.Input + o.Input, u.Output + o.Output, u.CacheWrite + o.CacheWrite, u.CacheRead + o.CacheRead}\ninternal/servecmd/reaping.go:67:\t\t// The job's model credentials are in the controller's environment.\ninternal/servecmd/reaping.go:108:\t// With no credential Auth is \"\": only the rate_limit_event and limit-message\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:52:\tToken     gitx.TokenFunc // mints a push and fetch credential; nil in tests\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:54:\t// Model is the credential agent jobs receive. The zero value means none\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:57:\tModel modelauth.Credential\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:323:\tModel     modelauth.Credential           // zero when no model credential is set; see deps.Model\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:495:// buildDeps is the production builder: credentials from the environment,\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:506:\tcred, err := modelauth.Resolve(modelauth.Mode(os.Getenv(modelauth.EnvMode)), claude.New(claude.Options{}).CredentialEnv, os.Getenv)\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:507:\tvar model modelauth.Credential\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:509:\tcase errors.Is(err, modelauth.ErrNoCredential):\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:511:\t\te.Log.Info(\"serve.model_credential_unset\", \"hint\", \"agent jobs will fail until one is set\", \"error\", err)\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:513:\t\treturn deps{}, fmt.Errorf(\"ard serve: model credential: %w\", err)\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:516:\t\te.Log.Info(\"serve.model_credential\", \"credential\", cred)\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:1:// Package modelauth picks the one model credential agent jobs receive and\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:17:// Mode selects the credential.\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:22:\tModeAuto       Mode = \"auto\" // oauth token if set, else API key\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:24:\tModeOAuthToken Mode = \"oauth\"\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:33:\t// ErrNoCredential means the selected variable is unset or empty.\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:34:\tErrNoCredential = errors.New(\"modelauth: credential not set\")\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:37:\tErrBadValue = errors.New(\"modelauth: credential value rejected\")\n3:261-An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n5:263-Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n20:278-**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n26:284-**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"97eea5e6-0b48-4970-80c9-c4184e55acd8","uuid":"bccbbe15-c85e-43c1-802e-ed0531c9de0f","timestamp":"2026-11-23T18:38:27.325Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"// Package limits is the controller's book of exhausted usage limits: the\n// reactive half of subscription limit handling (#81). When a job stops at a\n// limit, the Reaper's Finisher records it here and the pool starts no agent\n// job for the exhausted account or model family until it resets. Observed\n// readings, thresholds and status comments are #63's.\n//\n// The book is persisted in the controller database's meta table, so a pause\n// survives a restart. It never kills a running job.\npackage limits\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"log/slog\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/state\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\t// DefaultPause is the first pause when the CLI gave no reset time.\n\tDefaultPause = 30 * time.Minute\n\t// BackoffCap is the longest pause chosen without a reset time. Each\n\t// further stop with no reset doubles the pause up to this; a job that\n\t// succeeds resets it.\n\tBackoffCap = 4 * time.Hour\n\t// MaxPause clamps a reset time that is far away: a misread unit must not\n\t// block the account for years.\n\tMaxPause = 8 * 24 * time.Hour\n\n\tmetaKey = \"limits.exhausted\"\n)\n\n// Meta is the key-value store the book persists in (*state.Store).\ntype Meta interface {\n\tMeta(ctx context.Context, key string) (string, error) // errors.Is(err, state.ErrNotFound) = empty book\n\tSetMeta(ctx context.Context, key, value string) error\n}\n\n// entry is one exhausted family. Family \"\" is the whole account.\ntype entry struct {\n\tUntil int64 `json:\"until\"`          // Unix seconds\n\tStep  int   `json:\"step,omitempty\"` // consecutive stops with no reset time\n}\n\n// Book records which families are exhausted and until when.\ntype Book struct {\n\tmu      sync.Mutex\n\tentries map[string]entry\n\tmeta    Meta\n\tnow     func() time.Time\n\tlog     *slog.Logger\n}\n\n// Open loads the book. A missing key gives an empty book; a malformed value\n// logs WARN limits.corrupt and gives an empty book (the next Exhaust\n// overwrites it). Only a read error from meta is returned.\nfunc Open(ctx context.Context, m Meta, now func() time.Time, log *slog.Logger) (*Book, error) {\n\tif now == nil {\n\t\tnow = time.Now\n\t}\n\tif log == nil {\n\t\tlog = slog.Default()\n\t}\n\tb := &Book{entries: map[string]entry{}, meta: m, now: now, log: log}\n\tv, err := m.Meta(ctx, metaKey)\n\tswitch {\n\tcase errors.Is(err, state.ErrNotFound):\n\t\treturn b, nil\n\tcase err != nil:\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"open limits book: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tvar got map[string]entry\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(v), &got); err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Warn(\"limits.corrupt\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn b, nil\n\t}\n\tif got != nil {\n\t\tb.entries = got\n\t}\n\treturn b, nil\n}\n\nfunc backoff(step int) time.Duration {\n\td := DefaultPause\n\tfor i := 0; i < step && d < BackoffCap; i++ {\n\t\td *= 2\n\t}\n\treturn min(d, BackoffCap)\n}\n\n// Exhaust records that family (or the whole account when \"\") is out of quota\n// and returns the effective time it is blocked until.\n//\n// A resetsAt in the future is used as given, clamped to MaxPause. A zero or\n// past resetsAt means the CLI gave no reset: the pause is DefaultPause, then\n// doubles with each further stop to BackoffCap, but only once the previous\n// pause has run out. A stop recorded while the family is already paused (a job\n// that was running when the limit hit) changes nothing, so Exhaust is\n// idempotent. The later of the old and new times is kept, and the book is\n// persisted before Exhaust returns; on a persist error memory is unchanged.\nfunc (b *Book) Exhaust(ctx context.Context, family string, resetsAt time.Time) (time.Time, error) {\n\tb.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer b.mu.Unlock()\n\tnow := b.now()\n\told, had := b.entries[family]\n\toldUntil := time.Unix(old.Until, 0)\n\tnext := old\n\tvar until time.Time\n\tswitch {\n\tcase resetsAt.After(now):\n\t\tuntil = resetsAt\n\t\tif limit := now.Add(MaxPause); until.After(limit) {\n\t\t\tuntil = limit\n\t\t}\n\t\tnext.Step = 0\n// reaperLoop returns the Reaper loop, or nil when the executor cannot be built.\nfunc reaperLoop(w *wiring) controller.Loop {\n\t_, r, err := w.reaperParts()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tw.Log.Error(\"reaper.executor_unavailable\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\treturn r\n}\n\n// resumeCloseouts is the closeout-resume startup hook. It runs before the pool\n// is adopted, so it never touches the pool.\nfunc (w *wiring) resumeCloseouts(ctx context.Context) error {\n\t// A store that cannot be read means state is broken: fail startup.\n\tif _, err := w.limitsBook(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"closeout-resume: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\t_, r, err := w.reaperParts()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\t// Without an executor nothing runs jobs either; do not keep serve down.\n\t\tw.Log.Error(\"reaper.resume_skipped\", \"error\", err)\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\treturn r.ResumeAll(ctx)\n}\n\n// parseFunc reads a job's agent result from its stdout log, with the adapter\n// its role is configured for.\nfunc (w *wiring) parseFunc() reaper.ParseFunc {\n\treg, err := agent.NewRegistry(claude.New(claude.Options{}))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tpanic(err) // a duplicate adapter name is a programming error\n\t}\n\treturn func(ctx context.Context, cfg *config.Resolved, sj state.Job, h executor.Handle, exitCode int) (agent.Result, error) {\n\t\tif cfg == nil {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, errors.New(\"no config loaded\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar kind config.Kind\n\t\tswitch sj.Stage {\n\t\tcase closeout.StageTriage:\n\t\t\tkind = config.KindTriage\n\t\tcase closeout.StageWorker:\n\t\t\tkind = config.KindWorker\n\t\tcase closeout.StageReview:\n\t\t\tkind = config.KindReviewer\n\t\tdefault:\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, fmt.Errorf(\"stage %q has no agent\", sj.Stage)\n\t\t}\n\t\trole, ok := cfg.Role(kind, sj.Role)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, fmt.Errorf(\"role %s/%s is not configured\", kind, sj.Role)\n\t\t}\n\t\ta, err := reg.Get(role.Agent)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif h.StdoutPath == \"\" {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, errors.New(\"job has no stdout log\")\n\t\t}\n\t\tf, err := os.Open(h.StdoutPath)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn agent.Result{}, fmt.Errorf(\"open stdout log: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tdefer f.Close()\n\t\tres, err := a.Parse(f, exitCode)\n\t\tif errors.Is(err, agent.ErrNoResult) {\n\t\t\treturn res, nil // a killed job has no result event: the partial result is the answer\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn res, err\n\t}\n}\n93-// LimitHit says the run met a usage or rate limit. Consumers act on it only\n94-// when !Result.OK: a run that saw a rejection and then succeeded keeps it.\n95:type LimitHit struct {\n96-\tScope    LimitScope\n97-\tFamily   string    // \"opus\" | \"sonnet\" | \"\" (whole account or unknown)\n98-\tResetsAt time.Time // UTC; zero when the stream gave no machine-readable reset\n99-\tSignal   LimitSignal\n100-}\n101-\n102-// Usage is token counts. Missing or null fields count as 0.\n103-type Usage struct{ Input, Output, CacheWrite, CacheRead int64 }\n104-\n105-// Add returns the field-wise sum.\n106-func (u Usage) Add(o Usage) Usage {\n107-\treturn Usage{u.Input + o.Input, u.Output + o.Output, u.CacheWrite + o.CacheWrite, u.CacheRead + o.CacheRead}\ninternal/servecmd/reaping.go:67:\t\t// The job's model credentials are in the controller's environment.\ninternal/servecmd/reaping.go:108:\t// With no credential Auth is \"\": only the rate_limit_event and limit-message\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:52:\tToken     gitx.TokenFunc // mints a push and fetch credential; nil in tests\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:54:\t// Model is the credential agent jobs receive. The zero value means none\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:57:\tModel modelauth.Credential\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:323:\tModel     modelauth.Credential           // zero when no model credential is set; see deps.Model\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:495:// buildDeps is the production builder: credentials from the environment,\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:506:\tcred, err := modelauth.Resolve(modelauth.Mode(os.Getenv(modelauth.EnvMode)), claude.New(claude.Options{}).CredentialEnv, os.Getenv)\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:507:\tvar model modelauth.Credential\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:509:\tcase errors.Is(err, modelauth.ErrNoCredential):\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:511:\t\te.Log.Info(\"serve.model_credential_unset\", \"hint\", \"agent jobs will fail until one is set\", \"error\", err)\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:513:\t\treturn deps{}, fmt.Errorf(\"ard serve: model credential: %w\", err)\ninternal/servecmd/serve.go:516:\t\te.Log.Info(\"serve.model_credential\", \"credential\", cred)\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:1:// Package modelauth picks the one model credential agent jobs receive and\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:17:// Mode selects the credential.\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:22:\tModeAuto       Mode = \"auto\" // oauth token if set, else API key\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:24:\tModeOAuthToken Mode = \"oauth\"\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:33:\t// ErrNoCredential means the selected variable is unset or empty.\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:34:\tErrNoCredential = errors.New(\"modelauth: credential not set\")\ninternal/modelauth/modelauth.go:37:\tErrBadValue = errors.New(\"modelauth: credential value rejected\")\n3:261-An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n5:263-Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n20:278-**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n26:284-**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KcxgbB3mgSs3k8fFwswSZ2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
